    {
      title: 'Shade as Infrastructure',
      text: "Cities are hotter than the countryside around them, sometimes by several degrees. The reasons are well understood. Concrete, asphalt and dark roofs absorb heat during the day and release it slowly at night; buildings block the wind; and air conditioners, vehicles and machinery pump additional heat into the streets. Scientists call the result the urban heat island, and as global temperatures rise, it is becoming a serious public health problem rather than a minor inconvenience.\n\nThe people most affected are rarely those who make planning decisions. Outdoor workers — delivery riders, construction crews, street cleaners — have little choice about spending the hottest hours outside. Elderly residents, particularly those living alone, are vulnerable because the body's ability to regulate temperature declines with age, and because a hot night prevents the recovery that normally follows a hot day. In many cities, poorer neighbourhoods also have fewer trees, so the burden falls unevenly.\n\nTrees are among the most effective responses. A mature tree cools its surroundings in two ways: by blocking direct sunlight, and by releasing water vapour through its leaves, a process that absorbs heat from the air. Measurements on hot afternoons regularly show that the surface of a pavement under a full canopy can be well over ten degrees cooler than an exposed one nearby.\n\nThe difficulty is that trees are slow and demanding. A newly planted tree may take fifteen or twenty years to provide meaningful shade, and in that time it needs water, space for its roots, and protection from construction work. City budgets, by contrast, tend to favour projects whose benefits can be shown before the next election. Trees are also frequently treated as decoration — the first thing removed when a road is widened.\n\nA growing number of urban planners argue that this attitude must change, and that shade should be regarded as infrastructure, in the same category as drains or street lighting: something a city cannot function safely without, and which must be planned, funded and maintained accordingly.",
      questions: [
        { q: 'Which of these is given as a cause of the urban heat island?', options: ['Cities receive more sunlight than rural areas.', 'Hard surfaces absorb heat and release it slowly.', 'There is more rain in the countryside.', 'Cities are usually built at lower altitudes.'], answer: 1 },
        { q: 'Why are elderly residents especially at risk?', options: ['Their bodies control temperature less well, and hot nights prevent recovery.', 'They usually live in poorer neighbourhoods.', 'They spend more time working outdoors.', 'They refuse to use air conditioning.'], answer: 0 },
        { q: 'According to the passage, how do trees cool their surroundings?', options: ['By blocking the wind and storing heat', 'By absorbing heat through their roots', 'By providing shade and releasing water vapour', 'By reflecting sunlight back into the sky'], answer: 2 },
        { q: 'Why do city budgets tend not to favour trees?', options: ['Trees are more expensive than drains.', 'Their benefits take many years to appear.', 'Residents complain about falling leaves.', 'Trees damage street lighting.'], answer: 1 },
        { q: 'What does the writer mean by calling shade "infrastructure"?', options: ['It should be built from concrete.', 'It is mainly a decorative feature.', 'It should be provided by private companies.', 'It is an essential service that must be planned and funded.'], answer: 3 }
      ]
    }
